Atomic patterns take only registers of their own type.

std::atomic<double> and std::atomic<float> are lowered through the SI/DI atomic
optabs. Once optimisation keeps the value in a float register the operand arrives
as (subreg:DI (reg:DF)), which the generic register_operand accepts, and the
backend prints a subreg as the local beneath it: `i64.atomic.store' and
`i64.atomic.rmw.cmpxchg' were handed an f64 (and `i32.atomic.store' an f32), and
llvm-mc rejected the unit. -O0 never met it because every value goes through
memory there.

The atomic insns now use wasm_register_operand, which rejects a subreg of a
register of another type. The optab then copies the operand into a pseudo of the
pattern's mode, and that move is the existing *cast reinterpret pattern.

