@@ -434,6 +434,17 @@
 ;; `%i1' is the address and `%f1' its offset immediate, exactly as the non-atomic
 ;; *load and *store patterns above spell them: an atomic instruction's memory
 ;; argument has the same two parts as an ordinary one's.
+;;
+;; Every register operand is `wasm_register_operand' and not the generic
+;; `register_operand'.  The generic predicate accepts `(subreg:DI (reg:DF))', which
+;; is how the middle end hands over the bits of a `std::atomic<double>' once
+;; optimisation has kept the value in its float register, and this backend prints a
+;; subreg as the local under it -- so `i64.atomic.store' was given an f64 and the
+;; assembler refused the unit.  The stricter predicate rejects a subreg whose
+;; register is of another type, the optab then copies the operand into a fresh
+;; pseudo of the pattern's own mode, and that copy is the `*cast' reinterpret
+;; above.  At -O0 every value goes through memory, which is why only optimised
+;; builds ever met this.
 
